        <section id="what-is-a-unit-of-code?"  class="t-bg-primary">
          
            <h2>What is a unit of code?</h2>
              <p>Units are the smallest groups of code that can be maintained, executed, reused and tested independently. A unit can be almost anything such as a line of code.</p>
<p>In most programming languages it is a:</p>
<ul>
<li>function,</li>
<li>subroutine,</li>
<li>method,</li>
<li>property.</li>
</ul>
<p>A <em>unit test</em> is a way of testing the smallest piece of code that can be logically isolated in a system. Smaller tests give a granular view of how the code is performing and by being small test can be run fast. <em>We will be looking at testing later on in the course in Unit 4.</em></p>
<p>See if you can identify what might constitute a unit in the code examples from Session 2, and in the sections of code in the following pages.</p>

          
        </section>
        
    
     
    
  
     
    
  
  
