        <section id="what-is-wet-and-dry-code?" >
          
            <h2>What is WET and DRY code?</h2>
              <p>WET and DRY are acronyms used in software development to explain coding principles.</p>
<p>WET = 'Write Everything Twice'</p>
<p>DRY = 'Don't Repeat Yourself'</p>
<p>When writing a program, we want our code to be as easy to debug and understand as possible. We therefore use certain conventions when we write code to ensure this. We can illustrate this using a simple example. Let’s say that we want a program to print the ingredients for different pizzas to the console using C++. We could just write out all the ingredients for each pizza individually.</p>

          
        </section>
        
    
     
    
  
  
        <section id="wet-code" >
          
            <h2>WET code</h2>
              <p>When code is ‘WET’, code segments are repeated. All of our pizzas have some common ingredients, meaning that the 4 lines in our code from lines 6, 13, and 20 are repeated.</p>

              
              <div class="c-code"><pre class="language-cpp"><code class="language-cpp"><span class="highlight-line"><span class="token macro property"><span class="token directive-hash">#</span><span class="token directive keyword">include</span> <span class="token string">&lt;iostream></span></span></span><br><span class="highlight-line"><span class="token keyword">using</span> <span class="token keyword">namespace</span> std<span class="token punctuation">;</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line"></span><br><span class="highlight-line"><span class="token keyword">int</span> <span class="token function">main</span> <span class="token punctuation">(</span><span class="token punctuation">)</span> <span class="token punctuation">{</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line">   cout <span class="token operator">&lt;&lt;</span> <span class="token string">"Ham pizza ingredients: "</span> <span class="token operator">&lt;&lt;</span> endl<span class="token punctuation">;</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line">   cout <span class="token operator">&lt;&lt;</span> <span class="token string">"  Ham"</span> <span class="token operator">&lt;&lt;</span> endl<span class="token punctuation">;</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line">   cout <span class="token operator">&lt;&lt;</span> <span class="token string">"  Pizza base"</span> <span class="token operator">&lt;&lt;</span> endl<span class="token punctuation">;</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line">   cout <span class="token operator">&lt;&lt;</span> <span class="token string">"  Tomato sauce"</span> <span class="token operator">&lt;&lt;</span> endl<span class="token punctuation">;</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line">   cout <span class="token operator">&lt;&lt;</span> <span class="token string">"  Cheese"</span> <span class="token operator">&lt;&lt;</span> endl<span class="token punctuation">;</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line">  cout <span class="token operator">&lt;&lt;</span> <span class="token string">"  \n"</span> <span class="token operator">&lt;&lt;</span> endl<span class="token punctuation">;</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line"></span><br><span class="highlight-line">  cout <span class="token operator">&lt;&lt;</span> <span class="token string">"Sausage pizza ingredients: "</span> <span class="token operator">&lt;&lt;</span> endl<span class="token punctuation">;</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line">  cout <span class="token operator">&lt;&lt;</span> <span class="token string">"  Sausage"</span> <span class="token operator">&lt;&lt;</span> endl<span class="token punctuation">;</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line">  cout <span class="token operator">&lt;&lt;</span> <span class="token string">"  Pizza base"</span> <span class="token operator">&lt;&lt;</span> endl<span class="token punctuation">;</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line">  cout <span class="token operator">&lt;&lt;</span> <span class="token string">"  Tomato sauce"</span> <span class="token operator">&lt;&lt;</span> endl<span class="token punctuation">;</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line">  cout <span class="token operator">&lt;&lt;</span> <span class="token string">"  Cheese"</span> <span class="token operator">&lt;&lt;</span> endl<span class="token punctuation">;</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line">  cout <span class="token operator">&lt;&lt;</span> <span class="token string">"  \n"</span> <span class="token operator">&lt;&lt;</span> endl<span class="token punctuation">;</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line"></span><br><span class="highlight-line">  cout <span class="token operator">&lt;&lt;</span> <span class="token string">"Mushroom pizza ingredients: "</span> <span class="token operator">&lt;&lt;</span> endl<span class="token punctuation">;</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line">  cout <span class="token operator">&lt;&lt;</span> <span class="token string">"  Mushroom"</span> <span class="token operator">&lt;&lt;</span> endl<span class="token punctuation">;</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line">  cout <span class="token operator">&lt;&lt;</span> <span class="token string">"  Pizza base"</span> <span class="token operator">&lt;&lt;</span> endl<span class="token punctuation">;</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line">  cout <span class="token operator">&lt;&lt;</span> <span class="token string">"  Tomato sauce"</span> <span class="token operator">&lt;&lt;</span> endl<span class="token punctuation">;</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line">  cout <span class="token operator">&lt;&lt;</span> <span class="token string">"  Cheese"</span> <span class="token operator">&lt;&lt;</span> endl<span class="token punctuation">;</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line">  cout <span class="token operator">&lt;&lt;</span> <span class="token string">"  \n"</span> <span class="token operator">&lt;&lt;</span> endl<span class="token punctuation">;</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line"></span><br><span class="highlight-line"><span class="token keyword">return</span> <span class="token number">0</span><span class="token punctuation">;</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line"><span class="token punctuation">}</span></span></code></pre>
</div>

            
              <p>But let’s say we want to change one of the common ingredients for all of the pizzas (for example bbq sauce instead of tomato sauce.) With the WET code above, they would have to make changes in 3 different places. In a large program, there is a high risk of missing somewhere that changes should have been made.</p>

          
        </section>
        
    
     
    
  
     
    
  
  
        <section id="dry-code" >
          
            <h2>DRY code</h2>
              <p>The ‘DRY’ code principle aims to reduce repetition of any kind. In the code below, we add an extra function, basic_pizza(), which we can use to print to console all of the ingredients that are common. Now, we can replace the 4 lines of repeated code with just one function call. This way, if we want to change the base to bbq sauce, we only have to make changes at ONE place in the script, not 3 places as we would have to have done with our WET code.</p>

              
              <div class="c-code"><pre class="language-cpp"><code class="language-cpp"><span class="highlight-line"><span class="token macro property"><span class="token directive-hash">#</span><span class="token directive keyword">include</span> <span class="token string">&lt;iostream></span></span></span><br><span class="highlight-line"><span class="token keyword">using</span> <span class="token keyword">namespace</span> std<span class="token punctuation">;</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line"></span><br><span class="highlight-line"><span class="token keyword">int</span> <span class="token function">basic_pizza</span><span class="token punctuation">(</span><span class="token punctuation">)</span> <span class="token punctuation">{</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line">  cout <span class="token operator">&lt;&lt;</span> <span class="token string">"  Pizza base"</span> <span class="token operator">&lt;&lt;</span> endl<span class="token punctuation">;</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line">  cout <span class="token operator">&lt;&lt;</span> <span class="token string">"  Tomato sauce"</span> <span class="token operator">&lt;&lt;</span> endl<span class="token punctuation">;</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line">  cout <span class="token operator">&lt;&lt;</span> <span class="token string">"  Cheese"</span> <span class="token operator">&lt;&lt;</span> endl<span class="token punctuation">;</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line">  cout <span class="token operator">&lt;&lt;</span> <span class="token string">"  \n"</span> <span class="token operator">&lt;&lt;</span> endl<span class="token punctuation">;</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line"></span><br><span class="highlight-line">  <span class="token keyword">return</span> <span class="token number">0</span><span class="token punctuation">;</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line"><span class="token punctuation">}</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line"></span><br><span class="highlight-line"><span class="token keyword">int</span> <span class="token function">main</span> <span class="token punctuation">(</span><span class="token punctuation">)</span> <span class="token punctuation">{</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line">  cout <span class="token operator">&lt;&lt;</span> <span class="token string">"Ham pizza ingredients: "</span> <span class="token operator">&lt;&lt;</span> endl<span class="token punctuation">;</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line">  cout <span class="token operator">&lt;&lt;</span> <span class="token string">"  Ham"</span> <span class="token operator">&lt;&lt;</span> endl<span class="token punctuation">;</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line">  <span class="token function">basic_pizza</span><span class="token punctuation">(</span><span class="token punctuation">)</span><span class="token punctuation">;</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line"></span><br><span class="highlight-line">  cout <span class="token operator">&lt;&lt;</span> <span class="token string">"Sausage pizza ingredients: "</span> <span class="token operator">&lt;&lt;</span> endl<span class="token punctuation">;</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line">  cout <span class="token operator">&lt;&lt;</span> <span class="token string">"  Sausage"</span> <span class="token operator">&lt;&lt;</span> endl<span class="token punctuation">;</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line">  <span class="token function">basic_pizza</span><span class="token punctuation">(</span><span class="token punctuation">)</span><span class="token punctuation">;</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line"></span><br><span class="highlight-line">  cout <span class="token operator">&lt;&lt;</span> <span class="token string">"Mushroom pizza ingredients: "</span> <span class="token operator">&lt;&lt;</span> endl<span class="token punctuation">;</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line">  cout <span class="token operator">&lt;&lt;</span> <span class="token string">"  Mushroom"</span> <span class="token operator">&lt;&lt;</span> endl<span class="token punctuation">;</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line">  <span class="token function">basic_pizza</span><span class="token punctuation">(</span><span class="token punctuation">)</span><span class="token punctuation">;</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line"></span><br><span class="highlight-line"><span class="token keyword">return</span> <span class="token number">0</span><span class="token punctuation">;</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line"><span class="token punctuation">}</span></span></code></pre>
</div>

            
              <p>This has the advantages of being easier to read as well as only needing to be changed in one place.</p>

          
        </section>
        
    
     
    
  
  
        <section id="key-characteristics"  class="t-bg-primary">
          
            <h2>Key characteristics</h2>
              <p>So the key characteristics of WET code are:</p>
<ul>
<li>repeated code sections</li>
</ul>
<p>And the key characteristics of DRY code:</p>
<ul>
<li>code is <em>generalised</em> (by making it into a separate function, or another way) to avoid repetition</li>
</ul>
<p>However, it is not always possible to write DRY code, as it can be difficult in some languages, such as low-level machine languages and procedural languages (such as C and FORTRAN). But the key difference is that WET code may have repeated code sections whereas DRY code is generalised.</p>

          
        </section>
        
    
     
    
  
     
    
  
  
